// test3.cm - crash probe. The integer-literal lexer closure does
//   lex.slice().parse::<i64>().unwrap()
// so an out-of-range literal PANICS instead of producing a diagnostic:
//
//   cme lex test3.cm
//     -> thread 'main' panicked: called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err`
//        value: ParseIntError { kind: PosOverflow }
//     -> exit code 101, no tokens, no diagnostics
//
// The same applies to the most-negative value: `-9223372036854775808`
// panics because the literal part (9223372036854775808) overflows i64
// before the unary minus is ever applied. Whitepaper A.5 calls literal-
// boundary behavior "unspecified" - but unspecified must never mean panic.
//
// Because lexing is whole-file, this line aborts the ENTIRE run: any
// diagnostics from earlier lines are lost too. Keep it in its own file.

int boom = 99999999999999999999
